# No direct pushes to `main`

Agent-enforced, since GitHub branch protection may be unavailable.

<!-- core -->
Never `git push` to `main` — plain, `--force`, or via a refspec like `develop:main`. Land work on
`develop` or the project's integration branch; `main` is reached only through a PR merged on GitHub,
never a local merge pushed straight up. Tag releases on `main` after merging, using semver. Where a
repo's branch protection *is* configured, that is the primary enforcement and this rule sits
underneath it.

<!-- /core -->

## Why it is enforced at the agent level

Branch protection on `main` requires a paid plan for private repos on personal accounts, so it is
not always available server-side. Where it is not, enforce the same discipline at the agent level,
in every project, regardless of plan.

## The rule

- Never `git push` directly to `main` — plain, `--force`, or via a branch-rename refspec like
  `develop:main`. The refspec form is included explicitly because it is the one that looks like it
  is pushing a feature branch.
- Land changes on `develop`, or the project's integration branch, first.
- Promote `develop` → `main` only via a pull request (`gh pr create --base main --head develop`),
  merged through GitHub — never a local merge pushed straight to `main`.
- Tag releases on `main` after merging (`gh release create vX.Y.Z --generate-notes`, or an explicit
  `git tag` plus push), using semver.

## Where server-side protection exists

Do not
treat a configured ruleset as licence to stop checking; treat a missing one as the case this rule
was written for.

## Pushing at all

Commit or push only when the user asks. If you are on the default branch and about to commit, branch
first.

Applies globally, all projects, no exceptions, until the user says otherwise.
